{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792454822985,"sessionID":"ses_ea95e7f61ffeAWmqzYp3aSBPPN","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"webfetch","callID":"call_00_RCxC3TsV2tBNNOQ5kPsV8997","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"url":"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cusolverdx/get_started/functions/gtsv.html","format":"markdown"},"output":"  Solve General Tridiagonal System of Equations — cuSOLVERDx \n\n                 \n\n[Skip to main content](#main-content)\n\nBack to top Ctrl+K\n\n [![cuSOLVERDx - Home](../../_static/nvidia-logo-horiz-rgb-blk-for-screen.svg) ![cuSOLVERDx - Home](../../_static/nvidia-logo-horiz-rgb-wht-for-screen.svg)\n\ncuSOLVERDx](../../index.html)\n\nChoose version\n\nSearch Ctrl+K\n\n-   System Settings\n-   Light\n-   Dark\n\nSearch Ctrl+K\n\n [![cuSOLVERDx - Home](../../_static/nvidia-logo-horiz-rgb-blk-for-screen.svg) ![cuSOLVERDx - Home](../../_static/nvidia-logo-horiz-rgb-wht-for-screen.svg)\n\ncuSOLVERDx](../../index.html)\n\nChoose version\n\n-   System Settings\n-   Light\n-   Dark\n\nTable of Contents\n\n-   [Documentation Home](../../index.html)\n\nGetting Started\n\n-   [Requirements](../requirement.html)\n-   [Installation](../installation.html)\n-   [Functionality](../functionality.html)\n    \n    -   [Linear Solve](linear_solve.html)\n        \n        -   [Cholesky POTRF](potrf.html)\n        -   [Cholesky POTRS](potrs.html)\n        -   [Cholesky POSV](posv.html)\n        -   [LU GETRF](getrf.html)\n        -   [LU GETRS](getrs.html)\n        -   [LU GESV](gesv.html)\n        -   [LU GTSV\\_NO\\_PIVOT](#)\n        -   [MODIFIED\\_LU](modified_lu.html)\n        \n    -   [Least Squares](least_squares.html)\n        \n        -   [GELS](gels.html)\n        \n    -   [Orthogonal Factors](orthogonal_factor.html)\n        \n        -   [QR GEQRF](geqrf.html)\n        -   [QR UNMQR](unmqr.html)\n        -   [QR UNGQR](ungqr.html)\n        -   [LQ GELQF](gelqf.html)\n        -   [LQ UNMLQ](unmlq.html)\n        -   [LQ UNGLQ](unglq.html)\n        \n    -   [Symmetric Eigenvalues](symmetric_eigenvalues.html)\n        \n        -   [Symmetric/Hermitian Tridiagonal HTEV](htev.html)\n        -   [Symmetric/Hermitian HEEV](heev.html)\n        -   [Reduction from Generalized to Standard Form (HEGST)](hegst.html)\n        -   [Generalized Eigenvalue Solver (HEGV)](hegv.html)\n        \n    -   [Singular Value Decomposition (SVD)](svd.html)\n        \n        -   [BDSVD](bdsvd.html)\n        -   [GESVD](gesvd.html)\n        \n    -   [BLAS](blas.html)\n        \n        -   [Triangular System Solve TRSM](trsm.html)\n        \n    \n-   [Using cuSolverDx](../introduction.html)\n-   [Achieving High Performance](../performance.html)\n\nAPI Reference\n\n-   [Operators](../../api/operators.html)\n    \n    -   [Description Operators](../../api/description_ops.html)\n    -   [Execution Operators](../../api/execution_ops.html)\n    \n-   [Traits](../../api/traits.html)\n    \n    -   [Description Traits](../../api/description_traits.html)\n    -   [Execution Traits](../../api/execution_traits.html)\n    -   [Other Traits](../../api/other_traits.html)\n    \n-   [Execution Methods](../../api/methods.html)\n-   [I/O Methods](../../api/other_methods.html)\n-   [Shared Memory Management](../../api/other_shared.html)\n\nPython\n\n-   [Python Bindings](../../python_bindings.html)\n\nExamples\n\n-   [Examples List](../../examples/index.html)\n    \n    -   [Introduction](../../examples/intro_example.html)\n    -   [Linear Solve](../../examples/linear_solve_example.html)\n    -   [Least Squares](../../examples/least_squares_example.html)\n    -   [Orthogonalization](../../examples/orthogonal_factors_example.html)\n    -   [Symmetric Eigenvalue](../../examples/symmetric_eigenvalues_example.html)\n    -   [Singular Value Decomposition](../../examples/svd_example.html)\n    -   [BLAS](../../examples/blas_example.html)\n    -   [NVRTC](../../examples/nvrtc_example.html)\n    -   [Performance](../../examples/performance_example.html)\n    -   [Advanced](../../examples/advanced_example.html)\n    \n\nReferences\n\n-   [Release notes](../../release_notes.html)\n-   [GitHub Samples](https://github.com/NVIDIA/CUDALibrarySamples/tree/main/MathDx/cuSolverDx)\n-   [License](../../license.html)\n\nMathDx\n\n-   [cuSolverDx](https://developer.nvidia.com/cusolverdx-downloads)\n-   [cuBLASDx](https://developer.nvidia.com/cublasdx-downloads)\n-   [cuFFTDx](https://developer.nvidia.com/cufftdx-downloads)\n-   [cuRANDDx](https://developer.nvidia.com/curanddx-downloads)\n-   [nvCOMPDx](https://developer.nvidia.com/nvcompdx-downloads)\n\n-   [](../../index.html)\n-   [Functionality](../functionality.html)\n-   [Linear Solve A x = B](linear_solve.html)\n-   Solve General Tridiagonal System of Equations\n\n# Solve General Tridiagonal System of Equations[#](#solve-general-tridiagonal-system-of-equations \"Link to this heading\")\n\nGTSV\\_NO\\_PIVOT (General Tridiagonal system SolVe) function solves a system of linear equations for general tridiagonal matrices:\n\n\\\\\\[A X = B\\\\\\]\n\nwhere:\n\n-   \\\\(A\\\\) is the input batched \\\\(N \\\\times N\\\\) tridiagonal matrix, defined with three vectors `dl`, `d`, and `du`, corresponding to the subdiagonal, diagonal, and superdiagonal elements, respectively:\n    \n\n\\\\\\[\\\\begin{split}\\\\begin{bmatrix} d\\_0 & du\\_0 & & & \\\\\\\\ dl\\_0 & d\\_1 & du\\_1 & & \\\\\\\\ & dl\\_1 & \\\\ddots & \\\\ddots & \\\\\\\\ & & \\\\ddots & d\\_{N-2} & du\\_{N-2} \\\\\\\\ & & & dl\\_{N-2} & d\\_{N-1} \\\\end{bmatrix}\\\\end{split}\\\\\\]\n\n-   \\\\(X\\\\) is the output batched \\\\(N \\\\times K\\\\) solution matrix, and\n    \n-   \\\\(B\\\\) is the input batched \\\\(N \\\\times K\\\\) right-hand side matrix.\n    \n\ncuSolverDx `gtsv_no_pivot` function does not perform any pivoting and uses a combination of the Parallel Cyclic Reduction (PCR) and Thomas algorithms to solve the tridiagonal system of equations. The Thomas algorithm, based on LU decomposition, is efficient to solve tridiagonal systems but inherently sequential, while the PCR algorithm is more computationally expensive but parallelizable. The hybrid combination of the two algorithms is used to achieve the best performance for different problem sizes.\n\ncuSolverDx `gtsv_no_pivot` device functions (see [Execution Methods](../../api/methods.html#execute-method-label)):\n\n\\_\\_device\\_\\_ void execute(const data\\_type\\* dl, const data\\_type\\* d, const data\\_type\\* du, data\\_type\\* b, status\\_type\\* info);\n// with the runtime leading dimension\n\\_\\_device\\_\\_ void execute(const data\\_type\\* dl, const data\\_type\\* d, const data\\_type\\* du, data\\_type\\* b, unsigned int ldb, status\\_type\\* info);\n\n`dl` is a batched \\\\(N-1\\\\) array containing the subdiagonal elements of `A`, `d` is a batched \\\\(N\\\\) array containing the diagonal elements, and `du` is a batched \\\\(N-1\\\\) array containing the superdiagonal elements.\n\nNote\n\n`dl`, `d`, and `du` are unmodified by the functions. This is different from the Lapack `gtsv` functions (see [LAPACK gtsv documentation](https://www.netlib.org/lapack/explore-html/d6/ddb/group__gtsv.html)), which modifies the three arrays to store the factors of the matrix `A` after the LU factorization.\n\n`B` is a batched dense \\\\(N \\\\times K\\\\) right-hand side matrix, the leading dimension of `B` is \\\\(\\\\mathrm{ldb} \\\\geq N\\\\) if `B` is in column-major layout, or \\\\(\\\\mathrm{ldb} \\\\geq K\\\\) if `B` is row-major.\n\nThe output matrix `X` overwrites `B` with the same arrangement and leading dimension.\n\nThe output status parameter, `info`, is an array of batch size. If LU factorization is successful, every element of `info` is zero. If LU factorization failed for any batches, i.e. there was a division by zero, `info[batch_id] = i` indicates the `i-1`\\-th row produced a zero diagonal element.\n\nThe function supports:\n\n-   `B` being either column- or row-major memory layout, see [Arrangement operator](../../api/description_ops.html#arrangement-operator-label).\n    \n\nNote\n\nBecause matrix \\\\(A\\\\) in the functions is implicitly defined with three vectors, `dl`, `d`, and `du`, the leading dimension, or arrangement of \\\\(A\\\\), if defined, is ignored.\n\nNote\n\nThe functions do not support transposed matrix \\\\(A\\\\) via [TransposeMode operator](../../api/description_ops.html#transposemode-operator-label), however, the equation \\\\(A^T X = B\\\\) can be solved by interchanging the order of the arguments `dl` and `du`.\n\n[\n\nprevious\n\nLU Factorization and Solve\n\n\n\n](gesv.html \"previous page\")[\n\nnext\n\nModified LU Factorization for Unitary Matrices\n\n](modified_lu.html \"next page\")\n\n [![NVIDIA](../../_static/nvidia-logo-horiz-rgb-1c-blk-for-screen.svg) ![NVIDIA](../../_static/nvidia-logo-horiz-rgb-1c-wht-for-screen.svg)](https://www.nvidia.com)\n\n[Privacy Policy](https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/about-nvidia/privacy-policy/) | [Your Privacy Choices](https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/about-nvidia/privacy-center/) | [Terms of Service](https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/about-nvidia/terms-of-service/) | [Accessibility](https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/about-nvidia/accessibility/) | [Corporate Policies](https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/about-nvidia/company-policies/) | [Product Security](https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/product-security/) | [Contact](https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/contact/)\n\nCopyright © 2024-2026, NVIDIA Corporation & Affiliates. All rights reserved.","metadata":{"truncated":false},"title":"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cusolverdx/get_started/functions/gtsv.html (text/html)","time":{"start":1792454822672,"end":1792454822940}},"id":"prt_156a209da001qeJ7K77juu7vF7","sessionID":"ses_ea95e7f61ffeAWmqzYp3aSBPPN","messageID":"msg_156a187fc00150A1azsdkzPbG8"}}
