{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792454992406,"sessionID":"ses_ea95e7f61ffeAWmqzYp3aSBPPN","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"websearch","callID":"call_00_RPnu96IfRawAulh0DTxf3235","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"numResults":3,"query":"\"Fast Tridiagonal Solvers on the GPU\" \"28x speedup over the LAPACK solver\" \"512\""},"output":"{\n  \"search_id\": \"search_0fcb65b64cd966ec06b135bbd7af5117\",\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://research.nvidia.com/sites/default/files/pubs/2010-01_Fast-Tridiagonal-Solvers/Zhang_Fast_2009.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"[PDF] Fast Tridiagonal Solvers on the GPU - Research at NVIDIA\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2009-11-02\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Fast Tridiagonal Solvers on the GPU\\nTo combine the benefits of the basic algorithms, we propose hybrid CR+PCR and CR+RD al- gorithms, which improve the performance of PCR, RD and CR by 21%, 31% and 61% respectively. Our GPU solvers achieve up to a 28x speedup over a sequential LAPACK solver, and a 12x speedup over a multi-threaded CPU solver.\\nThe hybrid algorithms improve PCR, RD and CR by 21%, 31% and 61% respectively, and achieve a 12.5x speedup over a multi-threaded CPU solver. Third, we perform experiments to determine the accuracy of various algorithms and give sugges- tions for improving numerical stability.\\nFor solving 512 512-unknown systems, the hybrid solvers achieve a 12.5x speedup over the multi-threaded CPU solver, and a 28x speedup over the LAPACK solver. We recommend that the GPU solvers should be used as a part of a larger GPU applica- tion to amortize the PCI-Express transfer cost.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://research.nvidia.com/publication/2010-01_fast-tridiagonal-solvers-gpu\",\n      \"title\": \"Fast Tridiagonal Solvers on the GPU | Research\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"1. Publications\\n2. Fast Tridiagonal Solvers on the GPU\\nFast Tridiagonal Solvers on the GPU\\nWe study th