utations Next\\nSection 2 introduces background knowledge on the tridiagonal system solvers and details of their parallel implementation in PfSolve .\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE Permutation Predictions for Non-Clairvoyant Scheduling Previous ###### NEXT ARTICLE Communication Lower Bounds and Optimal Algorithms for Symmetric Matrix Computations Next\\n2 Tridiagonal Matrix Solvers\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE Permutation Predictions for Non-Clairvoyant Scheduling Previous ###### NEXT ARTICLE Communication Lower Bounds and Optimal Algorithms for Symmetric Matrix Computations Next\\nA diagonal + second sub and super diagonal solver is essentially a tridiagonal solver of two independent systems of size (\\\\frac{N}{2}) , so no new\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE Permutation Predictions for Non-Clairvoyant Scheduling Previous ###### NEXT ARTICLE Communication Lower Bounds and Optimal Algorithms for Symmetric Matrix Computations Next\\nIn this section, we will showcase the performance of the PTA in three scenarios: single-upload tridiagonal solver with varying number of RHS vectors,\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE Permutation Predictions for Non-Clairvoyant Scheduling Previous ###### NEXT ARTICLE Communication Lower Bounds and Optimal Algorithms for Symmetric Matrix Computations Next\\ntridiagonal solver for system sizes (\\\\in \\\\left[2^4,2^{28}\\\\right]) and multiple iterations of bidiagonal matrix multiplications and backsolves.\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE Permutation Predictions for Non-Clairvoyant Scheduling Previous ###### NEXT ARTICLE Communication Lower Bounds and Optimal Algorithms for Symmetric Matrix Computations Next\\nThe base scenario for a tridiagonal solver is a system of multiple RHS vectors solved against a single matrix using the algorithm from Section 2.3 .\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/1837853.1693472\",\n      \"title\": \"Fast tridiagonal solvers on the GPU | ACM SIGPLAN Notices\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Fast tridiagonal solvers on the GPU | ACM SIGPLAN Notices\\nWe study the performance of three parallel algorithms and their hybrid variants for solving tridiagonal linear systems on a GPU:\\nFast tridiagonal solvers on the GPU | ACM SIGPLAN Notices\\nWe study the performance of three parallel algorithms and their hybrid variants for solving tridiagonal linear systems on a GPU: cyclic reduction (CR), parallel cyclic reduction (PCR) and recursive doubling (RD).\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://dl.acm.org/doi/epdf/10.1145/3716171\",\n      \"title\": \"High Performance Solution of Tridiagonal Systems on the GPU\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"High Performance Solution of Tridiagonal Systems on the GPU\\nIn this article, we present PfSolve—a new, performant, cross-platform, and open-source implementation of tridiagonal and bidiagonal matrix solvers for the GPU architecture.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/1693453.1693472\",\n      \"title\": \"Fast tridiagonal solvers on the GPU | Proceedings of the 15th ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Fast tridiagonal solvers on the GPU | Proceedings of the 15th ...\\nWe study the performance of three parallel algorithms and their hybrid variants for solving tridiagonal linear systems on a GPU: cyclic reduction (CR), parallel cyclic reduction (PCR) and recursive doubling (RD).\\nFast tridiagonal solvers on the GPU | Proceedings of the 15th ...\\nWe study the performance of three parallel algorithms and their hybrid variants for solving tridiagonal linear systems on a GPU: cyclic reduction (CR), parallel cyclic reduction (PCR) and recursive doubling (RD).\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"http://impact.crhc.illinois.edu/shared/Papers/hee_icpp2011.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"A Scalable Tridiagonal Solver for GPUs - IMPACT\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"A Scalable Tridiagonal Solver for GPUs\\nHee-Seok Kim, Shengzhao Wu, Li-wen Chang and Wen-mei W. Hwu Electrical and Computer Engineering University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign Urbana, Illinois, USA { kim868,wu14,lchang20,w-hwu } @illinois.edu\\nAbstract —We present the design and evaluation of a scalable tridiagonal solver targeted for GPU architectures.\\nThe proposed method is scalable as it can cope with various input system sizes by properly adjusting algorithm trasition point. Our method on a NVidia GTX480 shows up to 8.3x and 49x speedups over multithreaded and sequential MKL implementations on a 3.33GHz Intel i7 975 in double precision, respectively.\\nKeywords -GPU Computing, GPGPU, Tridiagonal solver, Tridiagonal systems\\nI. INTRODUCTION\\nThe tridiagonal solver is an important core tool in wide range of engineering and scientific applications.\\nIn this paper, we propose a robust and scalable parallel tridiagonal solver that uses GPUs effectively. Our method is a hybrid of the tiled PCR and p-Thomas algorithms. It implements divide-and-conquer approach as tiled PCR breaks down a system into multiple independent systems, then p-Thomas takes over the systems and runs in parallel.\\nThe proposed method not only performs efficiently for large size systems but also covers arbitrary number of systems.\\nOur experiments show that the proposed method on a NVidia GTX480 can achieve up to 8.3x and 49x speedups over multithreaded and sequential MKL implementation on an 3.33GHz Intel i7 975 with double precision arithmetic.\\nWith single precision, we provide up to 12.9x and 82.5x speedups over the CPU implementations. The rest of the paper is organized as follows: Section II describes previous and background material about tridi- agonal solvers and GPU architectures that is relevant to this paper. Section III describes the proposed algorithm in detail.\\nIn contrast, our method shows close results compared to the CPU implementations when M is small, however, as M grows it outperforms demonstrating significant speedups.\\nOur method in double precision achieves up to 8.3x and 49x speedups over multi-threaded and sequential MKL library, respectively, when N is 512.\\n97–106, Aug. 2007.\\n[4] N. Sakharnykh, “Tridiagonal solvers on the GPU and ap- plications to fluid simulation,” NVIDIA GPU Technology Conference, September 2009.\\n[5] N. Sakharnykh, “Efficient tridiagonal solvers for ADI meth- ods and fluid simulation,” NVIDIA GPU Technology Confer- ence, September 2010.\\n[6] R. W.\\nOwens, “Fast tridiagonal solvers on the GPU,” in Proceedings of the 15th ACM SIGPLAN symposium on Principles and practice of parallel programming , PPoPP ’10, (New York, NY, USA), pp. 127– 136, ACM, 2010.\\n[17] Y. Zhang, J. Cohen, A. A. Davidson, and J. D.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://dl.acm.org/doi/fullHtml/10.1145/3472456.3472484\",\n      \"title\": \"Tridiagonal GPU Solver with Scaled Partial Pivoting at ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Tridiagonal GPU Solver with Scaled Partial Pivoting at ...\\nby C Klein · 2021 · Cited by 8 — This work incorporates scaled partial pivoting into a tridiagonal GPU solver in such a fashion that despite the data-dependent decisions no SIMD divergence\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://dlnext.acm.org/doi/pdf/10.1145/3716171\",\n      \"title\": \"High Performance Solution of Tridiagonal Systems on the GPU\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2025-05-13\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"High Performance Solution of Tridiagonal Systems on the GPU\\n \\nb 0\\n· · ·\\nb i\\n· · ·\\nb N −1\\n \\n \\n1 c 0 b 0 · · ·\\nai bi 1 ci bi\\n· · · aN −1 bN −1 1\\n \\n(19)\\nThe main diagonal can then be propagated either left or right:\\n \\n1 c 0 · · ·\\na i 1 c i\\n· · · a N −1 1\\n \\n \\nb 0\\n· · ·\\nb i\\n· · ·\\nb N −1\\n \\nTo solve a tridiagonal system with PfSolve, the user specifies\\n \\nb 0\\n· · ·\\nb i\\n· · ·\\nb N −1\\n \\n \\n1 c 0 b 0 · · ·\\nai bi 1 ci bi\\n· · · aN −1 bN −1 1\\n \\n(19)\\nThe main diagonal can then be propagated either left or right:\\n \\n1 c 0 · · ·\\na i 1 c i\\n· · · a N −1 1\\n \\n \\nb 0\\n· · ·\\nb i\\n· · ·\\nb N −1\\n \\nsolvers, copy kernels, inlined FFTs, read/write managers.\\n \\nb 0\\n· · ·\\nb i\\n· · ·\\nb N −1\\n \\n \\n1 c 0 b 0 · · ·\\nai bi 1 ci bi\\n· · · aN −1 bN −1 1\\n \\n(19)\\nThe main diagonal can then be propagated either left or right:\\n \\n1 c 0 · · ·\\na i 1 c i\\n· · · a N −1 1\\n \\n \\nb 0\\n· · ·\\nb i\\n· · ·\\nb N −1\\n \\nthree scenarios: single-upload tridi- agonal solver with varying\\n \\nb 0\\n· · ·\\nb i\\n· · ·\\nb N −1\\n \\n \\n1 c 0 b 0 · · ·\\nai bi 1 ci bi\\n· · · aN −1 bN −1 1\\n \\n(19)\\nThe main diagonal can then be propagated either left or right:\\n \\n1 c 0 · · ·\\na i 1 c i\\n· · · a N −1 1\\n \\n \\nb 0\\n· · ·\\nb i\\n· · ·\\nb N −1\\n \\nnumber of RHS vectors, tridiagonal solver for system sizes ∈\\n \\nb 0\\n· · ·\\nb i\\n· · ·\\nb N −1\\n \\n \\n1 c 0 b 0 · · ·\\nai bi 1 ci bi\\n· · · aN −1 bN −1 1\\n \\n(19)\\nThe main diagonal can then be propagated either left or right:\\n \\n1 c 0 · · ·\\na i 1 c i\\n· · · a N −1 1\\n \\n \\nb 0\\n· · ·\\nb i\\n· · ·\\nb N −1\\n \\nThe base scenario for a tridiagonal solver is a system of\\n \\nb 0\\n· · ·\\nb i\\n· · ·\\nb N −1\\n \\n \\n1 c 0 b 0 · · ·\\nai bi 1 ci bi\\n· · · aN −1 bN −1 1\\n \\n(19)\\nThe main diagonal can then be propagated either left or right:\\n \\n1 c 0 · · ·\\na i 1 c i\\n· · · a N −1 1\\n \\n \\nb 0\\n· · ·\\nb i\\n· · ·\\nb N −1\\n \\nSingle-upload benchmark of Nvidia H100 GPU in tridiagonal matrix\\n \\nb 0\\n· · ·\\nb i\\n· · ·\\nb N −1\\n \\n \\n1 c 0 b 0 · · ·\\nai bi 1 ci bi\\n· · · aN −1 bN −1 1\\n \\n(19)\\nThe main diagonal can then be propagated either left or right:\\n \\n1 c 0 · · ·\\na i 1 c i\\n· · · a N −1 1\\n \\n \\nb 0\\n· · ·\\nb i\\n· · ·\\nb N −1\\n \\nSystems < 512 use single-warp PTA, Systems ≥ 512 use\\n \\nb 0\\n· · ·\\nb i\\n· · ·\\nb N −1\\n \\n \\n1 c 0 b 0 · · ·\\nai bi 1 ci bi\\n· · · aN −1 bN −1 1\\n \\n(19)\\nThe main diagonal can then be propagated either left or right:\\n \\n1 c 0 · · ·\\na i 1 c i\\n· · · a N −1 1\\n \\n \\nb 0\\n· · ·\\nb i\\n· · ·\\nb N −1\\n \\nSingle-upload benchmark of AMD MI210 GPU in tridiagonal matrix\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.computer.org/csdl/proceedings-article/sc/2012/1000a071/12OmNCmGNZZ\",\n      \"title\": \"A scalable, numerically stable, high-performance tridiagonal solver using GPUs\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2012-11-01\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"1. Home\\n2. Proceedings\\n3. SC\\n4. SC 2012\\n  SC Conference\\nA scalable, numerically stable, high-performance tridiagonal solver using GPUs Year: 2012, Pages: 1-11\\nDOI Bookmark: 10.1109/SC.2012.12\\nAuthors\\nIn this paper, we present a scalable, numerically stable, high-performance tridiagonal solver. The solver is based on the SPIKE algorithm for partitioning a large matrix into small independent matrices, which can be solved in parallel.\\nFor each small matrix, our solver applies a general 1-by-1 or 2-by-2 diagonal pivoting algorithm, which is also known to be numerically stable. Our paper makes two major contributions. First, our solver is the first numerically stable tridiagonal solver for GPUs.\\nOur solver provides comparable quality of stable solutions to Intel MKL and Matlab, at speed comparable to the GPU tridiagonal solvers in existing packages like CUSPARSE. It is also scalable to multiple GPUs and CPUs.\\nSecond, we present and analyze two key optimization strategies for our solver: a high-throughput data layout transformation for memory efficiency, and a dynamic tiling approach for reducing the memory access footprint caused by branch divergence.\\nLike what you’re reading?\\nAlready a member? Sign In\\nNon-Member\\n36.00 Member\\n16.00\\nAdd to Cart Sign In\\n* A Stable Parallel Algorithm for Diagonally Dominant Tridiagonal Linear Systems 2015 IEEE 22nd International Conference on High Performance Computing (HiPC)\\n* New Tridiagonal Systems Solvers on GPU Architectures 2015 IEEE 22nd International Conference on High Performance Computing (HiPC)\\n* A Hierarchical Tridiagonal System Solver for Heterogenous Supercomputers 2014 5th Workshop on Latest Advances in Scalable Algorithms for Large-Scale Systems (ScalA)\\n* A Study on the Implementation of Tridiagonal Systems Solvers Using a GPU 2011 30th International Conference of the Chilean Computer Science Society\\n* Solving Multiple Tridiagonal Systems on a Multi-GPU Platform 2018 26th Euromicro International Conference on Parallel, Distributed and Network-based Processing (PDP)\\n* Solving tridiagonal systems on a GPU 2013 20th International Conference on High Performance Computing (HiPC)\\n* Solving Large Problem Sizes of Index-Digit Algorithms on GPU: FFT and Tridiagonal System Solvers IEEE Transactions on Computers\\n* A Hybrid Parallel Solving Algorithm on GPU for Quasi-Tridiagonal System of Linear Equations IEEE Transactions on Parallel & Distributed Systems\\n* A Novel Compute-Efficient Tridiagonal Solver for Many-Core Architectures IEEE Transactions on Parallel & Distributed Systems\\n* Scalable Many-Core Algorithms for Tridiagonal Solvers Computing in Science & Engineering\\nCopyright 2026 IEEE - All rights reserved.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"warnings\": null,\n  \"metadata\": null,\n  \"session_id\": \"ses_ea95e7f61ffeAWmqzYp3aSBPPN\"\n}","metadata":{"provider":"parallel","truncated":false},"title":"Parallel Web Search: \"Fast Tridiagonal Solvers on the GPU\" \"28x speedup over the LAPACK solver\" \"512\"","time":{"start":1792454991696,"end":1792454992401}},"id":"prt_156a49e24001q98NxMquuxiz86","sessionID":"ses_ea95e7f61ffeAWmqzYp3aSBPPN","messageID":"msg_156a40e22001IpPdrwhVGfsGI3"}}
