{"type":"text","timestamp":1792455005474,"sessionID":"ses_ea95e7f61ffeAWmqzYp3aSBPPN","part":{"id":"prt_156a4bcd0001KVpc3nIFfpLnmY","messageID":"msg_156a4a411001r1Sim2ciZ8ehYH","sessionID":"ses_ea95e7f61ffeAWmqzYp3aSBPPN","type":"text","text":"{\"items\":[{\"ref\":\"card:question\",\"status\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"page_quote\":\"\",\"note\":\"Research question posing what the plan will measure; it asks rather than asserts the T-vs-log2(T) depth claim, so no source is required.\"},{\"ref\":\"card:hypothesis\",\"status\":\"PARTIAL\",\"page_quote\":\"The Thomas algorithm, based on LU decomposition, is efficient to solve tridiagonal systems but inherently sequential, while the PCR algorithm is more computationally expensive but parallelizable.\",\"note\":\"s3 supports the parallel-vs-sequential contrast (Thomas sequential, PCR parallelizable) behind 'GPU width shortens the sequential part', but the page does not state the specific 'log2(T) rounds instead of T' figure.\"},{\"ref\":\"card:sequential_part\",\"status\":\"UNSUPPORTED\",\"page_quote\":\"\",\"note\":\"Factual claim that the block-bidiagonal Newton correction is a forward substitution with T ordered steps; it cites no source.\"},{\"ref\":\"card:method\",\"status\":\"PARTIAL\",\"page_quote\":\"The Thomas algorithm, based on LU decomposition, is efficient to solve tridiagonal systems but inherently sequential, while the PCR algorithm is more computationally expensive but parallelizable.\",\"note\":\"s3 confirms PCR is parallelizable, but the page does not describe cyclic reduction 'halving the system each round' or state 'log2(T) rounds' replacing T ordered steps.\"},{\"ref\":\"card:if_lever\",\"status\":\"UNSUPPORTED\",\"page_quote\":\"\",\"note\":\"Asserts as fact that cyclic-reduction wall-clock grows like log2(N) and beats sequential Thomas by a widening margin; no source cited.\"},{\"ref\":\"card:if_not\",\"status\":\"UNSUPPORTED\",\"page_quote\":\"\",\"note\":\"Asserts as fact that extra work and memory traffic dominate and one GPU has too few threads to hide them; no source cited.\"},{\"ref\":\"card:test_summary\",\"status\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"page_quote\":\"\",\"note\":\"Experiment design (what the plan will build and measure: timing Thomas vs PCR on one A100, N=2^10..2^24, checking against the gtsv reference); no source needed.\"},{\"ref\":\"card:closest_prior\",\"status\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"page_quote\":\"We find that CR enjoys linear algorithm complexity but suffers from more algorithmic steps and bank conflicts, while PCR and RD have fewer algorithmic steps but do more work each step. Our GPU solvers achieve up to a 28x speedup over a sequential LAPACK solver. We implement the five tridiagonal solvers on an NVIDIA GTX 280 GPU.\",\"note\":\"All sub-claims check out against the paper: it studies CR/PCR/RD, states PCR/RD have fewer steps but more work, gives a 28x speedup over sequential LAPACK, and implements on an NVIDIA GTX 280 GPU.\"},{\"ref\":\"card:left_open\",\"status\":\"UNSUPPORTED\",\"page_quote\":\"\",\"note\":\"Claim that no published result times PCR against an on-GPU sequential Thomas on a modern A100 up to N=2^24; no source cited for this literature-gap claim.\"},{\"ref\":\"card:anchor\",\"status\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"page_quote\":\"\",\"note\":\"Plan-defined correctness target (kernels must match NVIDIA's gtsv reference and a CPU dense solve to 1e-10 relative residual); an acceptance criterion, not a factual claim, so no source needed.\"},{\"ref\":\"card:limits\",\"status\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"page_quote\":\"\",\"note\":\"Scope/limitation statement describing what the plan shows (one tridiagonal solve's scaling on one modern GPU, not the end-to-end method); no source needed.\"},{\"ref\":\"src:s3\",\"status\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"page_quote\":\"The Thomas algorithm, based on LU decomposition, is efficient to solve tridiagonal systems but inherently sequential, while the PCR algorithm is more computationally expensive but parallelizable. The hybrid combination of the two algorithms is used to achieve the best performance for different problem sizes.\",\"note\":\"Quote appears verbatim. The page also states the function 'uses a combination of the Parallel Cyclic Reduction (PCR) and Thomas algorithms', matching the supports field (Thomas sequential, PCR parallel, NVIDIA ships a hybrid).\"},{\"ref\":\"src:s2\",\"status\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"page_quote\":\"We find that CR enjoys linear algorithm complexity but suffers from more algorithmic steps and bank conflicts, while PCR and RD have fewer algorithmic steps but do more work each step.\",\"note\":\"Quote appears verbatim in the paper's abstract, supporting 'PCR has fewer steps but more work per step'; the same paper also states the 28x LAPACK speedup proxy.\"},{\"ref\":\"num:speedup_at_nmax\",\"status\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"page_quote\":\"Our GPU solvers achieve up to a 28x speedup over a sequential LAPACK solver, and a 12x speedup over a multi-threaded CPU solver.\",\"note\":\"Page states 28x over sequential LAPACK (abstract), confirmed in body ('a 28x speedup over the LAPACK solver' for 512 512-unknown systems). Value 28 matches. (Abstract says 12x, body 12.5x, for the CPU solver, but the item's value is the 28x LAPACK figure.)\",\"page_value\":28},{\"ref\":\"num:per_gpu_usd_h_a100\",\"status\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"page_quote\":\"NVIDIA A100 SXM | 40 GB | 30 | 220 GiB | 512 GiB SSD | $1.99\",\"note\":\"The 1x (1-GPU) tab of the Instances pricing table lists NVIDIA A100 SXM 40 GB at $1.99/GPU/hr, matching the recorded value and the 1-GPU plan setting.\",\"page_value\":1.99}]}","time":{"start":1792454999248,"end":1792455005469}}}
